  "id": "Compound::DB00777 - Gene::146",
  "verbalization": "Propiomazine, a compound with a known ability to bind to Adra1a, appears to exert its effects through a shared pathway involving the Adrenergic Receptor Activity. This binding event is consistently observed across multiple paths, with scores indicating a strong, albeit slightly varying, level of confidence in the interaction. The observed pathway involves Adra1a participating in the Alpha1- and Alpha-adrenergic Receptor Activities, which are both subtypes of the broader Adrenergic Receptor Activity. Adra1d, as a receptor that participates in these activities, is thus positioned as a downstream target of Adra1a. The integration of these pathways suggests that Propiomazine may modulate Adra1d through a mechanism involving Adra1a, potentially influencing the function of the adrenergic receptor complex. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Propiomazine and Adra1d, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
